`env_0029_retail_energy_supplier` is finished and passes everything: `validate_scenario` prints **VALID** with 5 difficulties, 10 of 10 tasks loading, 64 tools and 387 knowledge-base documents. All three delivery conditions hold: every difficulty passed the task audit, every rendered product passed the document audit, and the whole scenario loads as one.

The company is Beacon Ridge Energy, a competitive electricity and gas supplier in PA, OH, IL, NJ and MD. It has 7 products and 45 plans.

| Difficulty | Product | Task audit | Doc audit | Docs |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| residential_electricity_care | power | passed in round 2 | passed in round 1 | 86 |
| residential_gas_care | gas | passed in round 2 | passed in round 2 | 68 |
| small_business_electricity_care | bizpower | passed in round 3 | passed in round 3 | 67 |
| commercial_gas_care | bizgas | passed in round 2 | passed in round 1 | 54 |
| cni_contract_care | cni | passed in round 2 | passed in round 1 | 73 |
| *(no difficulty)* | solar, protect | — | not audited (by design) | 20 + 19 |

- **Gates:** I re-ran every gate myself at the end and all are green, including the traceability check that can only run once the documents exist.
- **Shared identifiers:** customers in different difficulties share no identifier. I checked 146 identity values and 848 record ids across all ten tasks.
- **First-round audit results:** all five difficulties failed their first task audit. In most tasks the auditor's independent working still reached the expected answers. The failures were in the design of the policies:
  - pricing adjustments that could never apply to any eligible customer, or were mislabelled;
  - rules that overrode a base clause without saying so;
  - one task where two different orders of steps were both allowed and gave different credits.

  The original authors fixed these, and the original auditors confirmed the fixes.

**Things to know before using it:**
- **Some figures are invented.** Many of the bonus, discount and adjustment ladders inside the computations are company rules the task authors made up. The skills require 4–6 adjustment factors per computation. They pass every audit, but real suppliers usually use flatter credits than these.
- **Known issues left in place.** None changes a scored answer, and none was marked as a failure, so I logged them in `worklog.md` rather than sending them back:
  - Gas: the care agent's credit limit is worded in a way that allows two readings when a credit is close to $150.00; neither gas task comes near that.
  - Gas: some base rules were retired entirely, but their replacements only cover the narrower cases the tasks use, so ordinary cases have no rule in force.
  - Small business electricity: the termination fee schedule still lists an old basis for estimating remaining usage, which contradicts the calculation.
  - Commercial gas: one task has an electricity bill line posted the day after the task's "today".
- **Re-audits were not blind.** The same auditor checked each round, so from round 2 on it had already seen the expected answers. Every audit file says so.
- **solar and protect were never audited.** No difficulty uses them, so only their own gates stand behind them.
- **The session was interrupted once** by the account usage limit. The round-2 task re-audits had not started writing anything, so I sent them again without redoing any finished work.
- **Steps ran in parallel batches rather than one at a time.** The agent tool here waits for a whole batch to finish, so each difficulty moved to its next stage when its batch returned. I logged this decision in `worklog.md`.

I deleted my temporary audit views and staging folders. `tasks/cni_contract_care/worklog.md` is that task author's own log and contains answers, so keep it out of anything an agent being tested can read.

Everything is in `env/env_0029_retail_energy_supplier/`; `worklog.md` there has a timestamped line for every start, finish, round and decision.
